---
/**
 * hospitality / HowBK — "Villa Marindu" HOW IT WORKS (design element
 * data-component="HOW-IT-WORKS").
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one procedural band. On a villa with no booking engine this
 * is the only place that says how a stay is actually arranged — in the owner's
 * words, never ours. The design draws it as the panel-toned band between the
 * gallery and the testimonials: eyebrow, one Prata headline, three equal
 * columns each opened by a brass-outlined circled numeral, and a single brass
 * button under them.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html":
 *   band     background #141A18 + 1px #262E2B top and bottom  → .band-panel
 *   padding  92px 0
 *   eyebrow  10px / .44em caps / #B99552                      → .kick
 *   h2       Prata 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px), margin .26em 0 52px, max 24ch
 *   grid     repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 44px
 *   numeral  42x42, 1px #B99552, 50% radius, Prata 17px #B99552 → .fig
 *   h3       Prata 400, 25px, margin 18px 0 10px, #F4F1EA
 *   body     15px / 1.75 / #A9AFA9, margin 0                   → .body
 *   action   margin-top 52px, btnBrass = brass fill + #101413 label,
 *            12px / .16em caps, 17px 32px, 2px radius           → .btn.btn--fill
 * Every one of those colours is a wrapper token (R1); not a hex is written here.
 * btnBrass is character-for-character the wrapper's .btn--fill, so this file
 * adds NO button rule at all — only the 100% width and white-space at 520px.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (all declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "how" — a data-field on any other path is a dead hook)
 *   how.tag                  — the eyebrow
 *   how.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place the template's
 *                              signature italic-brass <em> anywhere inside
 *                              their own sentence. Guarded: derive-content-
 *                              defaults supplies NO how.headline, so an unset
 *                              value must draw nothing, not a gap above the grid
 *   how.steps.${i}.title
 *   how.steps.${i}.body      — canonical key, spelled in the hook even when the
 *                              stored value arrived as .desc / .description, so
 *                              the first edit heals the row onto the schema key
 *   how.cta.text             — the brass button's label, THIS band's own
 *   how.cta.href             — its target. The schema declares how.cta.text as a
 *                              link FieldSpec, so hrefPath how.cta.href is owned
 *                              too. It used to be hero.cta1.*, shared with the
 *                              header, the hero and the footer: one string for
 *                              four buttons, so retyping one retyped them all —
 *                              and one colour too, because the v3 picker derives
 *                              a button's SECTION from the leading segment of its
 *                              field path and answered "hero" for all four. On
 *                              how.cta this band is retypable and recolourable on
 *                              the How-it-works scope alone. Both halves FALL
 *                              BACK to hero.cta1.* while how.cta is unset, so no
 *                              existing site loses a button; the hooks always
 *                              spell how.cta, so the first edit diverges the band
 *
 * DERIVED, not content:
 *   The circled numeral. It counts DISPLAYED steps (01, 02, 03 — never a gap)
 *   and is aria-hidden with no data-field, so the design's demo `st.n` can never
 *   become a content key an owner has to maintain. The list is a real <ol>, so
 *   the ordinal reaches assistive tech through the markup rather than through a
 *   decorative glyph.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The design's
 * own steps ("Message me", "I hold the dates", "48 hours") are the DESIGNER
 * filling in their own template — they are a reply-time and a hold-length
 * promise made for a business nobody has spoken to, so none of them ships. A
 * villa that has said nothing about its process gets no process band: blank rows
 * are dropped and an all-blank list removes the whole <section>.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5) = rows.length. It counts the BODY content only. It cannot
 * count how.tag, because derive-content-defaults hard-codes how.tag to 'How it
 * works' on EVERY site — a gate that counted it would ship an eyebrow and a
 * band over an empty grid on every site that never filled the steps in.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field carries the ORIGINAL array index, the numeral
 * carries the DISPLAY index. Dropping a blank row 2 therefore renumbers nothing
 * and cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2.
 *
 * ARRAY SOURCE. how.steps is canonical, and the hook ALWAYS spells how.steps —
 * the read-tolerant / write-canonical split every other How section in the repo
 * uses. A legacy site whose rows live under how.items still RENDERS, but its
 * hooks are not re-pointed at how.items: genericContentSchema declares no
 * how.items list and no fallbackPaths for it, so isSchemaEditablePath refuses
 * `how.items.N.title` outright and a hook spelled that way would be inert in the
 * v3 sidebar. Spelling how.steps keeps the row editable and heals it onto the
 * canonical array on the first save.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - No id on the section. This page's anchors are about / services / gallery /
 *     location / faq / main, and the design gives HOW-IT-WORKS no id and no nav
 *     link. Inventing #how here would add a target nothing in the page points at.
 *   - No step count is asserted anywhere in the markup or the copy. The grid is
 *     three tracks because the design is three tracks, but two or five owner-
 *     typed steps lay out on the same tracks without a hole or a wrap.
 *
 * THEMING (R1/R4). --accent is the only token the theme engine may move in this
 * template, and here it touches the numeral ring, the numeral, the eyebrow and
 * the button fill — all of which pair with --on-accent or sit on --night-2, so
 * a re-picked accent stays legible. The section class is "how-band" and the
 * button is .btn--fill (double dash): buildOverrideCss pins label colours on
 * .btn-primary / .btn-yellow / .nav-cta / .testi .btn / .cta-band .btn / footer
 * .btn with !important, and none of those selectors can reach anything here.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped <style>, no is:global. The headline is fed with
 * set:html, and scoped rules cannot reach elements a set:html creates — which is
 * why there is no italic-brass rule in this file: PageBK already styles
 * `:where(.h2 em)` from its global block, so an owner's <em> is emphasised there
 * and a scoped duplicate here would be dead CSS.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBK passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const how = content.how || {};
const tag = typeof how.tag === 'string' ? how.tag.trim() : '';
// Trimmed, and otherwise untouched: this goes out through set:html, so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's em rule intact.
const headline = typeof how.headline === 'string' ? how.headline.trim() : '';

type Step = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
// Read tolerantly — a legacy site stores its rows under how.items — and write
// canonically. See ARRAY SOURCE above.
const source: Step[] = Array.isArray(how.steps) ? how.steps : (Array.isArray(how.items) ? how.items : []);
const titleOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.body ?? s?.desc ?? s?.description ?? '').trim();
// A step the admin added but never filled would otherwise print a brass ring
// around a numeral over nothing, and a title of pure whitespace would ship an
// empty <h3>. Normalise first, drop the blanks (whitespace counts as blank) and
// keep the ORIGINAL index on every survivor.
const rows = source
  .map((s, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(s), body: bodyOf(s) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// Anchor guard (R7). derive-content-defaults hard-codes hero.cta1.href to
// '#visit', an id this page does not render, so a button that falls back to it
// would scroll nowhere. Resolve in-page hrefs against the ids PageBK renders and
// keep this table in step with the copies in HeaderBK / HeroBK / CtaBandBK /
// FooterBK. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass straight through.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['about', 'services', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  amenities: 'services',
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'services',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};
// THIS BAND'S OWN button. how.cta is read FIRST; the page's hero action is the
// FALLBACK, and the fallback half is exactly what this file read before the
// split — so a site that never sets how.cta renders identically and no button is
// lost. The hooks always spell how.cta, which is what makes the first edit
// diverge instead of retyping the header, the hero and the footer with it.
const ctaText = how.cta?.text || hero.cta1?.text || '';
// #location is where this template puts the address and the way to reach the
// owner, so it is the honest landing place for an unresolvable contact action.
const ctaHref = onPage(how.cta?.href || hero.cta1?.href, '#location');
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="how-band band-panel">
    <div class="wrap">
      {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="how.tag">{tag}</span>}
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 how-h2 reveal" data-field="how.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}

      <ol class="how-grid">
        {rows.map((step, k) => (
          <li class="how-step reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {/* DERIVED chrome: display index, aria-hidden, no data-field. The
                <ol> already carries the ordinal for assistive tech. */}
            <span class="fig how-num" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {step.title && <h3 data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.title`}>{step.title}</h3>}
            {step.body && <p class="body" data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.body`}>{step.body}</p>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      {ctaText && (
        <div class="how-act reveal">
          <a class="btn btn--fill" href={ctaHref} data-field="how.cta.text" data-href-field="how.cta.href">{ctaText}</a>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* The band ground, the hairline edges and the panel tone all come from the
     wrapper's .band-panel. The only thing overridden here is the design's own
     92px block padding, which is tighter than the shared section rhythm; it
     scales down on the same curve so it never crushes on a phone.

     margin-top is the design's own `margin-top:100px` on this element. In this
     design EVERY section is padding-TOP-only with a bare bottom, so the leading
     gap belongs to the section that wants it — and this band is the one that
     asks for 100px. It cannot be borrowed from whatever renders above: GALLERY
     ends at 0 per its own design element, and any section can be switched off,
     so a band that leaned on a sibling's padding would butt straight into the
     mosaic. Scaled on the same curve PageBK uses for the design's other 100px.
     A margin (not padding) so the panel ground still starts below the gap. */
  .how-band { margin-top: clamp(60px, 8vw, 100px); padding: clamp(60px, 8vw, 92px) 0; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .44em tracking: one
     unbroken word would otherwise be the widest thing on the page (R8). */
  .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: margin .26em 0 52px. The wrapper's .h2 is a :where() rule, so this
     wins on specificity without !important. */
  .how-h2 { margin: 0.26em 0 52px; }

  /* derive-content-defaults ships how.tag on every site and NO how.headline, so
     "eyebrow, then straight into the grid" is the DEFAULT state rather than an
     edge case — and the h2's own margins are what normally open that gap.
     Adjacency, so it fires only when the headline is absent. */
  .kick + .how-grid { margin-top: 34px; }

  /* Design: repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) / gap 44px. minmax(0,1fr) rather than 1fr
     so a long unbroken word in a step cannot widen its track and push the page
     sideways. */
  .how-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 44px;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* min-width:0 as well as the minmax() above: a grid item's default min-width
     is auto, so one unbroken word would still widen its own track. */
  .how-step { list-style: none; min-width: 0; }

  /* Design: 42x42, 1px brass ring, full circle, Prata 17px brass. The face,
     colour and tabular figures come from the wrapper's .fig. */
  .how-num {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: 17px;
  }

  /* Prata ships ONE weight (400), so the weight is pinned rather than inherited
     from whatever heading rule is in scope — asking for a bolder one only makes
     the browser synthesise a faux bold. The design states NO line-height here,
     so its 25px Prata title renders at the face's normal leading (~1.15);
     PageBK's shared heading rule is 1.1, which would set a two-line title
     tighter than it was drawn, so the drawn leading is restated. */
  .how-step h3 {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 25px;
    line-height: 1.14;
    margin: 18px 0 10px;
    color: var(--bone-hi);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Qualified with the parent class so it beats the wrapper's bare-element
     paragraph reset, which is (0,1,1). */
  .how-step p.body { margin: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .how-act { margin-top: 52px; }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design, and they
     go straight to ONE at 900px rather than through a two-up: at 900 each track
     is about 246px, a ~30-character measure for a four-line step, and three
     steps in two tracks strand a lone orphan on the second row. WhyBK — the
     same three-track / 44px grid two sections up this page — folds at the same
     point for the same reasons, so the two bands stay in step on a tablet.
     Stacked, the paragraph takes a measure cap instead: a step should not run
     to 110 characters just because its column is now the whole page. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .how-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 32px; }
    .how-h2 { margin-bottom: 40px; }
    .how-step p.body { max-width: 62ch; }
  }
  /* 520 · tighten the stack. Nothing here may push the page sideways: the
     overflow-wrap rules above and the minmax/min-width tracks cover the text,
     and the action goes full-bleed below. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .how-grid { gap: 26px; }
    .how-h2 { margin: 0.26em 0 32px; }
    .kick + .how-grid { margin-top: 24px; }
    .how-num { width: 38px; height: 38px; font-size: 16px; }
    .how-step h3 { font-size: 21px; margin: 15px 0 9px; }
    .how-act { margin-top: 34px; }
    /* Full-bleed action: the wrapper pins .btn to white-space: nowrap, which
       would push the viewport sideways on a long owner-typed label. */
    .how-act .btn { width: 100%; white-space: normal; }
  }
</style>
